            {"id": "b", "text": "EPS12V 8-pin to PCIe 8-pin adapter, then insert only 6 of the 8 pins", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. EPS12V 8-pin connectors are CPU power connectors, and their pinout (4×12V + 4×GND for CPU) differs from PCIe 8-pin (6×12V + 2×GND for GPU). Adapting EPS12V to PCIe power risks incorrect voltage delivery. EPS and PCIe 8-pin connectors are keyed differently precisely to prevent this mistake."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "SATA power to PCIe 6-pin adapter, which provides sufficient power through the SATA power connector", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. While SATA-to-PCIe adapters exist, SATA power connectors are rated for 54W (4.5A at 12V). A PCIe 6-pin connector may need up to 75W. SATA-to-PCIe adapters are generally not recommended because the SATA cable gauge and connector are not rated for GPU power loads; they can melt or cause data corruption on other SATA devices sharing that cable."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "No adapter is needed; the PCIe slot itself provides all required power to the GPU without an auxiliary connector", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. A PCIe x16 slot provides up to 75W through the slot. If the GPU has a 6-pin connector, it requires additional power beyond what the slot provides (typically 75W slot + 75W per 6-pin = up to 150W total). Without the 6-pin connector, the GPU will throttle, fail to initialize, or trigger a low-power warning."},
